ircular sources by widely separated arrays. The conventional two-step methods extract measurement parameters and then, estimate the positions from them. Compared with the conventional two-step methods, direct position determination (DPD) is a promising technique, which locates transmitters directly from original sensor outputs without estimating intermediate parameters in a single step, and thus, improves the location accuracy and avoids the data association problem. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDirect position determination of cyclostationary signals / by Alit Mendelson-Reuven Alit | Scholarly Article | NNL_ALEPH990026122990205171 | The National Library of Israel (https://www.nli.org.il/en/dissertations/NNL_ALEPH990026122990205171/NLI)\nciteturn200search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; # Direct position determination of cyclostationary signalsMendelson-Reuven, Alit ...   * Signal detection\n\n# Direct position determination of cyclostationary signals\n\nMendelson-Reuven, Alit\n\n  * Scholarly Article\n\n  * Location & Availability\n\n  * Save\n\n##### More information\n\nTitle\n---\nAdditional Titles\n---\nRelated place\n---\nPublisher\n---\nCreation Date\n---\nNotes\n---\nExtent\n---\nLanguage\n---\nNational Library system number\n---\n\n##### Possible uses\n\nTerms of Use:\n\nProhibition of Copying\n\nThe item may be subject to copyright and/or terms of agreement. It may be prohibited to copy and use of the item for purposes of reproduction, publication, distribution, public performance, broadcasting, dissemination via the internet or by any other means, and creating a derivative work of the item (for example, translation, modification or adaptation) in any form or by any means, including digital or analog media, without prior agreement of the copyright owner and/or the owner of the collection.\n\n##### MARC RECORDS\n\n##### Tags\n\n  * Random noise theory\n  * Signal detection\n\nHave more information? Found a mistake? [Button: Help us complete missing information]\n\n## More on this subject\n\nAn innovation approach to random fields : application of white noise theory / Takeyuki Hida, Si Si. Book\n\nNoise sustained patterns : fluctuations and nonlinearities / Markus Loecher. Book\n\nNoise-induced phenomena in the environmental sciences / Luca Ridolfi, Paolo D'Odorico, Francesco Laio. Book\n\nFinite sample performance of ridge regularized least squares estimation in sub-Gaussian noise Michael Krikheili. Michael Krikheili author Scholarly Article\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDirect position determination of cyclostationary signals - Tel Aviv University (https://cris.tau.ac.il/en/publications/direct-position-determination-of-cyclostationary-signals/)\nciteturn200search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.9 years ago; Crawled: last month; Reuven, AM & Weiss, AJ 2009, 'Direct position determination of cyclostationary signals', Signal Processing, vol. 89, no. 12, pp.\n\n# Direct position determination of cyclostationary signals\n\n  * Alit Mendelson Reuven^{*}\n  * , Anthony J. Weiss\n\n^{*}Corresponding author for this work\n\n  * Department of Electrical Engineering - Systems\n\n  * Tel Aviv University\n\nResearch output: Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review\n\n54 Scopus citations\n\n## Abstract\n\nDirect position determination (DPD) was recently proposed as a new method for passive localization of narrowband radio transmitters. Its superior performance, especially in the low signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) regime, is ascribed to both the joint optimization it performs over all base stations that participate in the estimation process and the direct determination of the emitter position as opposed to conventional methods which typically process the information from the individual base stations separately and determine the location using two estimation steps. In this paper, we introduce the cyclic direct position determination (CDPD) algorithm, a novel method which is the cyclostationary counterpart of the DPD. Despite its name, the CDPD is not a simple extension of the DPD but a unique algorithm which deviates substantially from its ancestor. Yet, the CDPD enjoys both the benefits of the DPD algorithm and the great performance robustness in the presence of narrowband interferences thanks to its cyclostationarity exploitation.\n\nOriginal language  | English\nPages (from-to)  | 2448-2464\nNumber of pages  | 17\nJournal  | Signal Processing\nVolume  | 89\nIssue number  | 12\nDOIs  | \n\n  * https://doi.org/10.1016/j.sigpro.2009.04.009\nState  | Published - Dec 2009\n\n## Keywords\n\n  * Angle of arrival (AOA)\n  * Array processing\n  * Cyclostationarity\n  * Emitter localization\n  * Time of arrival (TOA)\n\n## Access to Document\n\n  * 10.1016/j.sigpro.2009.04.009\n\n## Fingerprint\n\nDive into the research topics of 'Direct position determination of cyclostationary signals'. Together they form a unique fingerprint.\n\nView full fingerprint\n\nReuven, A. M., & Weiss, A. J. (2009). Direct position determination of cyclostationary signals. Signal Processing, 89(12), 2448-2464. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.sigpro.2009.04.009\n\nReuven, Alit Mendelson ; Weiss, Anthony J. / Direct position determination of cyclostationary signals. In: Signal Processing. 2009 ; Vol. 89, No. 12. pp. 2448-2464.\n\n@article{cf68464be0c64195b2c265da066a739c,\n\ntitle = \"Direct position determination of cyclostationary signals\",\n\nabstract = \"Direct position determination (DPD) was recently proposed as a new method for passive localization of narrowband radio transmitters. Its superior performance, especially in the low signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) regime, is ascribed to both the joint optimization it performs over all base stations that participate in the estimation process and the direct determination of the emitter position as opposed to conventional methods which typically process the information from the individual base stations separately and determine the location using two estimation steps. In this paper, we introduce the cyclic direct position determination (CDPD) algorithm, a novel method which is the cyclostationary counterpart of the DPD. Despite its name, the CDPD is not a simple extension of the DPD but a unique algorithm which deviates substantially from its ancestor. Yet, the CDPD enjoys both the benefits of the DPD algorithm and the great performance robustness in the presence of narrowband interferences thanks to its cyclostationarity exploitation.\",\n\nkeywords = \"Angle of arrival (AOA), Array processing, Cyclostationarity, Emitter localization, Time of arrival (TOA)\",\n\nauthor = \"Reuven, \\{Alit Mendelson\\} and Weiss, \\{Anthony J.\\}\",\n\nyear = \"2009\",\n\nmonth = dec,\n\ndoi = \"10.1016/j.sigpro.2009.04.009\",\n\nlanguage = \"אנגלית\",\n\nvolume = \"89\",\n\npages = \"2448--2464\",\n\njournal = \"Signal Processing\",\n\nissn = \"0165-1684\",\n\npublisher = \"Elsevier B.V.\",\n\nnumber = \"12\",\n\n}\n\nReuven, AM & Weiss, AJ 2009, 'Direct position determination of cyclostationary signals', Signal Processing, vol. 89, no. 12, pp. 2448-2464. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.sigpro.2009.04.009\n\nDirect position determination of cyclostationary signals. / Reuven, Alit Mendelson; Weiss, Anthony J.\nIn: Signal Processing, Vol. 89, No. 12, 12.2009, p. 2448-2464.\n\nResearch output: Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review\n\nTY - JOUR\n\nT1 - Direct position determination of cyclostationary signals\n\nAU - Reuven, Alit Mendelson\n\nAU - Weiss, Anthony J.\n\nPY - 2009/12\n\nY1 - 2009/12\n\nN2 - Direct position determination (DPD) was recently proposed as a new method for passive localization of narrowband radio transmitters. Its superior performance, especially in the low signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) regime, is ascribed to both the joint optimization it performs over all base stations that participate in the estimation process and the direct determination of the emitter position as opposed to conventional methods which typically process the information from the individual base stations separately and determine the location using two estimation steps. In this paper, we introduce the cyclic direct position determination (CDPD) algorithm, a novel method which is the cyclostationary counterpart of the DPD. Despite its name, the CDPD is not a simple extension of the DPD but a unique algorithm which deviates substantially from its ancestor. Yet, the CDPD enjoys both the benefits of the DPD algorithm and the great performance robustness in the presence of narrowband interferences thanks to its cyclostationarity exploitation.\n\nKW - Angle of arrival (AOA)\n\nKW - Array processing\n\nKW - Cyclostationarity\n\nKW - Emitter localization\n\nKW - Time of arrival (TOA)\n\nUR - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/67651115655\n\nU2 - 10.1016/j.sigpro.2009.04.009\n\nDO - 10.1016/j.sigpro.2009.04.009\n\nM3 - ???researchoutput.researchoutputtypes.contributiontojournal.article???\n\nAN - SCOPUS:67651115655\n\nSN - 0165-1684\n\nVL - 89\n\nSP - 2448\n\nEP - 2464\n\nJO - Signal Processing\n\nJF - Signal Processing\n\nIS - 12\n\nER -\n\nReuven AM, Weiss AJ. Direct position determination of cyclostationary signals. Signal Processing. 2009 Dec;89(12):2448-2464. doi: 10.1016/j.sigpro.2009.04.009--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHigh Resolution Direct Detection ... preview & related info | Mendeley (https://www.mendeley.com/catalogue/6137f3aa-4c32-338a-98a8-ae55511d52a2/)\nciteturn200search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; # High Resolution Direct Detection and Position Determination of Sources with Intermittent Emission ...   * IEEE Access (2019) 7 43428-43437 ... Hao, K., & Wan, Q.\nJournal Article OPEN ACCESS\n\n# High Resolution Direct Detection and Position Determination of Sources with Intermittent Emission\n\n  * Hao K\n  * Wan Q\n  * IEEE Access (2019) 7 43428-43437\n\nDOI: 10.1109/ACCESS.2019.2907136\n\n19 Citations\n\nCitations of this article\n\n5 Readers\n\nMendeley users who have this article in their library.\n\nAdd to library\n\nView PDF\n\nThis article is free to access.\n\n  * Abstract\n  * Keywords (5)\n\n#### Abstract\n\nThis paper presents our research findings on the direct detection and position determination (DDPD) problem, which is to estimate the positions and the total number of multiple sources with intermittent emission, using the received signals intercepted by a moving antenna array at multiple observation slots. We combine the direct position determination (DPD) concept and the Minimum Variance Distortionless Response (MVDR) criterion to solve the DDPD problem without the prior knowledge of the effective number of sources (the number of sources at every observation slot). We find out that the parallel combination of the MVDR spectrum in the cost function results in its own sensitivity to the missing emission, which makes the location estimator bias. Therefore, we use the K-means clustering algorithm to identify the sources with intermittent emission and their emitting slots adaptively, based on the MVDR spectrum values over all observation slots. Then, we use the spectrum values of the identified emitting slots to reconstruct the MVDR spectrum. Finally, we find the peaks of the reconstructed MVDR spectrum, which are corresponding to the source positions. The results of the simulations demonstrate that the proposed method gets asymptotic performance with the signal to noise ratio (SNR), both in the aspects of the root mean square error (RMSE) and the bias error, and high resolution as a generalized MVDR based method.\n\n#### Author supplied keywords\n\n  * Direct position determination\n  * array processing\n  * maximum likelihood\n  * minimum variance distortionless response (MVDR)\n  * passive localization\n\nImage\n\n#### Cite\n\nCITATION STYLE\n\nAPA\n\n[Input]\n\nHao, K., & Wan, Q. (2019